def longest_bitonic_length(nums: list[int]) -> int:
    if not nums:
        return 0

    n = len(nums)
    inc = [1] * n  # increasing subsequence length
    dec = [1] * n  # decreasing subsequence length

    # calculate increasing subsequence length
    for i in range(1, n):
        for j in range(i):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                inc[i] = max(inc[i], inc[j] + 1)

    # calculate decreasing subsequence length
    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):
        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                dec[i] = max(dec[i], dec[j] + 1)

    # find the length of the longest bitonic subsequence
    return max(inc[i] + dec[i] - 1 for i in range(n))
```

This function uses dynamic programming to solve the problem. It first calculates the length of the longest increasing subsequence ending at each position, and then calculates the length of the longest decreasing subsequence starting at each position. The length of the longest bitonic subsequence is then the maximum of the sum of the lengths of the longest increasing and decreasing subsequences minus 1 (to exclude the peak element).

