def tokenize_code(source: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    tokens = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(source):
        if source[i].isdigit():
            j = i + 1
            while j < len(source) and (source[j].isdigit() or source[j] == '.'):
                j += 1
            tokens.append(('NUMBER', source[i:j]))
            i = j
        elif source[i].isalpha():
            j = i + 1
            while j < len(source) and source[j].isalnum():
                j += 1
            tokens.append(('NAME', source[i:j]))
            i = j
        elif source[i] in '+-*/<>=(),':
            tokens.append(('OP', source[i]))
            i += 1
        else:
            raise ValueError('Invalid character in source code')
    return tokens
```

This function works by iterating over the characters in the source code string. If the character is a digit, it finds the end of the number and appends a 'NUMBER' token to the list. If the character is a letter, it finds the end of the name and appends a 'NAME' token to the list. If the character is an operator, it appends an 'OP' token to the list. If the character is neither a digit, letter, nor an operator, it raises a ValueError.

